<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">companion gear</ansi>

Your companions wear what you give them. Better gear is the plainest way to
buff a companion: a sturdier weapon, heavier armor, a shield. Everything comes
from your shared cargo, and everything you take off goes back to it.

  <ansi fg="command">company equip [member] [item]</ansi>
  Gives one cargo item to a member (#1, #2, a name, or me for yourself). The
  gear it replaces goes back to cargo, so nothing is lost or duplicated.

  <ansi fg="command">company remove [member] [slot]</ansi>
  Takes a worn piece off and returns it to cargo.

  <ansi fg="command">company compare [member] [item]</ansi>
  Shows the change before you commit: protection, weapon hands and reach,
  burden and company capacity.

  <ansi fg="command">company gear [member]</ansi>
  What a companion wears and carries, and what it does for them: damage,
  protection, burden and dodge. <ansi fg="command">company status</ansi> carries a one-line gear summary for each.

The same rules apply to a companion as to you. A class refuses gear it isn't
taught (<ansi fg="command">help shields</ansi>), armor heavier than its training costs it skill and dodge
(<ansi fg="command">help armor</ansi>), and gear with a level requirement needs a member who has the level
(<ansi fg="command">help rarity</ansi>). Changes are refused in battle, and the companion must be alive and
with you. When a change lands, the reply lists what moved, such as
"Now: protection 3 -> 7, damage 1d4 -> 1d6+1".

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">When a companion leaves</ansi>

Dismiss a companion (<ansi fg="command">company dismiss</ansi>), lose one to desertion, or fail to raise a fallen one in time,
and every piece you gave them comes back to your cargo, with the report in
the same message. They keep only what they came with (their own weapon and
armor), so recruiting the same companion again never earns you free gear. If
the return leaves your company over capacity you can still walk; drop or sell
something, or give a pack horse more to carry (<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>).

A fallen companion keeps its gear while it waits to be raised.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Dolls</ansi>

A Doll Master's dolls are dressed with <ansi fg="command">doll wield</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">doll wear</ansi> (<ansi fg="command">help doll</ansi>). If their
Master leaves the company, what you dressed them in comes back to your cargo
too, except the cudgel every doll starts with.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">In the browser</ansi>

Company > Inventory lists every member's slots, empty ones too. Tap a slot to
pick from the cargo items that fit it, or tap worn gear to take it off. On a
computer you can also drag a cargo item onto a member's slot, and drag worn
gear back onto the cargo. Character > Gear previews Current -> After for any
member: choose who from the row of names at the top.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help equipment</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help doll</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help armor</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help shields</ansi>.
